/**
\page CrashDumpFacilityPage Crash Dump Facility

\tableofcontents

\section CrashDumpFacilityPage_Overview Overview

Every mitk::BaseApplication based application (MITK Workbench, MITK
FlowBench) arms an out-of-process crash handler at startup: a hard crash
(access violation, abort, stack overflow) leaves a Crashpad minidump in a
per-user directory, and the next start of the application offers to keep the
dump for reporting or to discard it.

The facility is built on
<a href="https://github.com/getsentry/sentry-native">sentry-native</a>
(superbuild option \c MITK_USE_sentry, on by default), which builds and ships
getsentry's fork of Crashpad. The library is linked statically and only by
the \c MitkCrashHandling module; a second target linking it would duplicate
process-global handler state, so all consumers go through
mitk::CrashDumpFacility.

\section CrashDumpFacilityPage_HardCrash What a hard crash looks like

What the user sees after a hard crash differs per platform, and only Windows
ends the process by itself. The dump is written and complete either way.

On Windows the out-of-process handler terminates the client once it has the
dump, so the application simply disappears.

On Linux there is no equivalent mechanism. Crashpad installs an in-process
signal handler, writes the dump synchronously from inside it while every
thread is stopped, and returns - it does not end the process, and it restores
whatever handler it replaced (in a MITK application that is Poco's, installed
earlier during mitk::BaseApplication construction). The faulting instruction
then re-executes and the second fault lands in a handler that has nothing left
to do, so the application stops responding instead of exiting. The user must
terminate it. macOS likewise leaves the process behind for the user to
terminate.

Terminating loses nothing: the minidump is on disk and complete before the
process stops responding, and it records the original fault - a SIGSEGV stays
a SIGSEGV in the dump, whatever signal the process dies from afterwards.

\section CrashDumpFacilityPage_Content What a dump contains

A minidump contains the stacks of all threads, CPU context, the list of
loaded modules, and memory ranges referenced by the stacks - not the full
heap. Its size is therefore governed by thread count and module count, not
by loaded image or patient data (a minimal process produces roughly 130 kB).
Stack-referenced memory can still include fragments of session data,
possibly patient data, which is why dumps are treated as sensitive and are
discarded by default in the next-start dialog.

Whether a given piece of data appears depends on whether it was on a thread
stack or in a register at capture time - not on whether it was loaded:

| Data | Where it lives | In the dump? |
| ---- | -------------- | ------------ |
| Patient name / ID in node or image properties (`mitk::StringProperty`) | `std::string` on the heap | At rest, usually not - only a pointer would be on a stack |
| Image pixel data | large heap allocations | No |
| An identifier being actively processed at the crash point | arguments, locals, registers, stack | Plausibly yes |
| Short strings held inline (`std::string` small-string optimization) | inline in a stack local | Yes, if such a local is live |
| File paths during I/O (DICOM filenames often embed a name or ID) | local string | Likely, if the crash is during loading |
| `QString` contents in general | heap; only a pointer on the stack | Usually not |

A dump must be assumed to possibly contain fragments of whatever the
application was handling during the session, not only at the moment of
capture: stack slots and registers can still hold values left over from
earlier work, and memory they point to is captured too. It will not hold the
bulk of loaded data, and there is no way to verify what it does hold. The
user-facing texts therefore make the rule conditional on the session: if it
ever touched non-anonymized data about real people (including DICOM browser
listings and PACS queries), the dump has to be handled like that data. Even
otherwise, the organisation's own rules (confidential or unpublished data) may
restrict sharing it, so the decision to pass a dump on is left explicitly with
the user.

The copy of the session log kept with a dump (see
\ref CrashDumpFacilityPage_RunInfo) needs the same care, and more directly: it
is plain text and records, among other things, the paths of the files the
session opened, which often embed a name or an ID. Unlike the dump, it can be
read and checked, so the user-facing texts point to it explicitly.

\section CrashDumpFacilityPage_LocalOnly Local-only by construction

Nothing is ever uploaded: no DSN is compiled in and no configuration surface
can set one; the crash handler is started with an empty upload URL, so it has
no endpoint to upload to; and sentry-native's own event transport is compiled
out (\c SENTRY_TRANSPORT=none), so no HTTP client is even linked. The stock
\c crashpad_handler still contains upload code - it is simply never handed an
endpoint - so the accurate statement is "never uploads", not "physically
cannot".

\section CrashDumpFacilityPage_Storage Where dumps live

The crash-dump database is
<tt>&lt;generic user data location&gt;/&lt;organization&gt;/&lt;application&gt;/CrashDumps</tt>
(on Windows typically under \c \%LOCALAPPDATA\%). Unlike the CTK framework
storage directory, the path carries no per-install hash: the hash would
require a QCoreApplication, which deliberately does not exist yet when the
facility arms (see below). All builds and installs of the same application
therefore share one database; the module list inside a dump still identifies
the exact binaries.

The facility keeps the newest <tt>maxDumpsPerKind</tt> dumps per area (crash
reports, on-demand snapshots, hard-killed freezes; at most 10, see
\ref CrashDumpFacilityPage_Settings); older ones are pruned at start-up, and
Crashpad prunes its own report area on its age/size schedule as well. The
primary cleanup, however, is the next-start dialog: dumps the user does not
explicitly keep are deleted immediately.

\section CrashDumpFacilityPage_Settings Settings

The persistent settings (mitk::CrashDumpSettings) live in
<tt>mitk-settings.json</tt> in the database directory, not in the MITK
preferences:

<table>
<tr><th>Key</th><th>Range</th><th>Default</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
<tr><td><tt>enabled</tt></td><td>bool</td><td>true</td><td>Arm the handler.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>maxDumpsPerKind</tt></td><td>1-10</td><td>10</td><td>Retention per dump area.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>watchdogTimeoutSeconds</tt></td><td>0, or 10-3600</td><td>0 (off)</td><td>UI-freeze watchdog timeout.</td></tr>
</table>

They are read by mitk::CrashDumpFacility::Initialize() and take effect at the
next start. Preferences would not do: arming happens before Qt and the
preference service exist, and a preference is per install (and temporary in
\c --BlueBerry.newInstance sessions) while the file has the scope of the
dumps it governs. A missing file means the defaults; an unreadable file, a key
of the wrong type or an out-of-range value falls back to the default or is
clamped, with a warning - the facility never fails start-up over its settings.
The Workbench and FlowBench edit the file through the
<i>Reports and Diagnostics > Crash Dumps</i> preference page
(mitk::CrashDumpFacility::WriteSettings()).

Precedence at start-up is command line, then environment variable, then
settings file: \c --MITK.no-crash-dumps and \c MITK_NO_CRASH_DUMPS disable
crash dumps even when <tt>enabled</tt> is true, and \c --MITK.ui-watchdog
and \c MITK_UI_WATCHDOG replace the stored watchdog timeout (with any value,
including 0, and without clamping, so that a debugging session can use a short
timeout). mitk::ResolveCrashDumpSessionOptions() implements this rule;
mitk::GetCrashDumpSessionOptions() tells the preference page which override is
active.

\section CrashDumpFacilityPage_Snapshots Non-fatal snapshots and dump kinds

Besides the crash dumps the handler writes automatically, the facility can
capture a minidump of the running process without crashing it, via
mitk::CrashDumpFacility::CaptureSnapshot (Crashpad's DumpWithoutCrash - a
full, all-thread dump). Not available on macOS, whose Crashpad client has no
DumpWithoutCrash (mitk::CrashDumpFacility::SupportsSnapshots()). Three kinds
of dump are tracked so the next-start dialog surfaces only the right ones:

- <b>Crash dumps</b> - written by the handler on a hard crash. Surfaced.
- <b>On-demand snapshots</b> - the "Capture diagnostics" button on the
  top-level error dialog (mitk::SnapshotKind::OnDemand). Shown once with
  their location, kept, and never re-surfaced.
- <b>Provisional freeze snapshots</b> - written by the UI-freeze watchdog
  (mitk::SnapshotKind::WatchdogProvisional). Purged when the freeze recovers
  or on clean shutdown, so only a hard-killed freeze leaves one behind.

The kind is made a filesystem fact that survives a hard kill: CaptureSnapshot
moves the freshly written dump out of Crashpad's report area into a
facility-owned subdirectory (<tt>mitk-snapshots</tt> or
<tt>mitk-pending-freeze</tt>). The surfacable set (mitk::CrashDumpFacility::ListDumps)
is therefore crash dumps plus <tt>mitk-pending-freeze</tt> survivors;
<tt>mitk-snapshots</tt> is excluded. The snapshot areas themselves are
enumerable per kind via mitk::CrashDumpFacility::ListSnapshots, so diagnostic
tooling can inspect them without knowing the directory layout.

mitk::CrashDumpFacility::ListAllDumps() lists every dump the user can handle,
each with its mitk::DumpKind (Crash, UnresponsiveTerminated, OnDemand, derived
from the area it is filed in) and its run info. The provisional snapshots of
the running session are left out there, as well as from the surfacable set and
from the acknowledgment watermark: they are deleted automatically when the
freeze recovers, and a slow start-up that briefly trips the watchdog must not
show up as a terminated session.

\subsection CrashDumpFacilityPage_Snapshots_Watchdog UI-freeze watchdog

The watchdog is opt-in and silent. A Qt-free mitk::HeartbeatMonitor runs a
worker thread that watches a heartbeat counter; QmitkUiFreezeWatchdog bumps
that counter from a QTimer on the UI thread. Detection begins with the first
heartbeat, not with the monitor: plugin loading routinely outlasts the timeout
before the event loop exists to beat at all, and a thread that has never
reported in has not stalled. If the UI thread then wedges, the timer stops
firing; after the configured timeout the monitor captures up to three
provisional snapshots a few seconds apart, so an analyst can tell a permanent
wedge from a slow loop. If the heartbeat resumes, the provisional
snapshots from that episode are purged - so a temporary freeze leaves nothing
behind and never prompts on the next start. Only a freeze that ends in a hard
kill (the out-of-process handler already wrote the dumps) survives.

It is off by default - legitimate long UI-thread operations exist, and false
positives would erode trust. Enable it with a timeout in seconds in the
settings file (the Crash Dumps preference page), or for one session via the
environment variable \c MITK_UI_WATCHDOG or the command-line option
\c --MITK.ui-watchdog (see \ref CrashDumpFacilityPage_Settings for the
precedence). Modal dialogs run a nested event loop and keep the heartbeat
going, so a dialog left open is not a freeze.

\section CrashDumpFacilityPage_Wiring Application wiring

mitk::BaseApplication::run() initializes the facility first thing, before
Poco option parsing and before any Qt application object exists - crashes
during the Qt/CTK startup window are a common and important crash class.

The handler executable is shipped as \c MitkCrashHandler (renamed from
upstream's generic \c crashpad_handler so it is recognizable as MITK's in a
task manager). Because the name differs from what the sentry backend would
auto-resolve, the facility sets \c handler_path explicitly to
<tt>&lt;directory of the current executable&gt;/MitkCrashHandler</tt>.

Which directory that is depends on the platform, so three rules place the
handler and all three have to agree: the \c MitkCrashHandling module copies it
into the runtime output directory, where the plain binaries are started from;
mitkFunctionCreateBlueBerryApplication copies it into <tt>Contents/MacOS</tt>
of every macOS application bundle, where the bundled executable is started
from instead; and the install rules ship one copy per installed bin directory.
Handler name and source file are defined once in the top-level
\c CMakeLists.txt, and the name reaches the facility as a compile definition,
so what is placed and what the facility spawns cannot drift apart.

Because arming happens before option parsing, the facility can only be
disabled through the settings file, the raw command line or the environment:

- <tt>"enabled": false</tt> in <tt>mitk-settings.json</tt>
- command-line flag \c --MITK.no-crash-dumps
- environment variable \c MITK_NO_CRASH_DUMPS

An application <tt>.ini</tt> file cannot gate the facility; its options are
parsed only after the facility is armed. This is a documented limitation.
Disabled or not, the facility is initialized (the flag and the variable pass
mitk::CrashDumpFacility::Config::Arm = false), so that the dumps already on
disk stay listable and deletable in a session that records no new ones.

If a run crashed, the next start shows QmitkCrashDumpDialog (hooked into the
workbench window advisors of the Workbench and FlowBench). The trigger is a
scan for dumps newer than the last acknowledged one - not sentry's
crashed-last-run marker, which is experimental API and unreliable under the
single-instance argument forwarding of \c setSingleMode(true); the marker is
only used as a corroborating signal. Dumps are surfaced exactly once:
<i>Keep for later</i>, <i>Remove</i> (also on closing the dialog) and
<i>File report...</i> all acknowledge them.

All dumps, kept or new, are handled in QmitkCrashDumpManagerDialog, a
non-modal dialog opened from <i>Help > Diagnostic Data...</i>, from the
preference page and from the "Capture diagnostics" result. It is a dialog of
its own rather than a preference page because deleting is immediate and
cannot be undone by the preference dialog's Cancel.

\section CrashDumpFacilityPage_RunInfo Run info per dump

Every dump records the session it came from in mitk::CrashRunInfo: the
release string, the installation directory and the session's log file. At
arming time the facility writes a per-process file
(<tt>&lt;temp&gt;/mitk-crash-run-info-&lt;pid&gt;/mitk-run-info.json</tt>; per
process because the database is shared by all running instances) and
registers it as a Crashpad attachment. The handler copies attachments into a
report when it writes the dump, reading them at that moment, so the log path
that QmitkCrashDumpDialog::ShowIfCrashedLastRun() adds once the log is open
(mitk::CrashDumpFacility::SetSessionLogFile()) reaches every later dump.
A dump taken before that point carries an empty log path.

The recorded path alone would not do: mitk::LogBackend rotates
<tt>mitk-0.log</tt> .. <tt>mitk-9.log</tt> on every start, so on the next
start of the same install the name already belongs to the new session. The
facility therefore keeps a copy of the log next to the dump
(mitk::CrashDumpInfo::SessionLog). A snapshot copies the log as written so far
at capture time. A crash dump gets its copy when it is first seen, normally in
the next Initialize() of the crashed install, which runs before the logs are
rotated; a log written after the dump is taken to be a later session's and is
not copied, so such a dump simply has no log.

The facility then keeps the run info next to the dump as
<tt>&lt;dump file name&gt;.json</tt> and the log copy as
<tt>&lt;dump file name&gt;.log</tt>: snapshots take both at capture, before
their report is released, crash dumps when they are first seen. Deleting or
pruning a dump removes both. The sidecar layout is internal;
the contract is mitk::CrashDumpInfo::RunInfo, which stays empty for dumps from
before this mechanism, and on platforms where the attachment does not arrive
(verified on Windows; Linux shares the code path; macOS is unexplored).

\section CrashDumpFacilityPage_ReportService Filing a report

The crash-dump UI does not send anything itself. A report feature registers an
mitk::ICrashReportService micro service; while one is registered, the
next-start dialog, the diagnostic data manager and the "Capture diagnostics"
result offer <i>File report...</i>, which hands the chosen dumps to the
service and leaves them on disk. Without a registered service the button is
not shown anywhere. The interface lives in \c MitkAppUtil rather than in the
Qt-free \c MitkCrashHandling because it takes a parent widget.

\section CrashDumpFacilityPage_Processes The handler process and multiple instances

Each armed application spawns one \c MitkCrashHandler process as a child; it
runs for the lifetime of the application and writes the dump out of process
when the application crashes. In the default single-instance mode
(\c setSingleMode(true)) a second launch forwards its arguments to the running
instance and exits, so no extra handler lingers. Genuinely concurrent
instances (for example started with \c --BlueBerry.newInstance) each get their
own handler against the shared database; Crashpad supports a shared database
via file locking, so this is safe.

\section CrashDumpFacilityPage_Debugger Running under a debugger

A crash-dump handler and an attached debugger compete for the same
exceptions, and on Windows the debugger wins. When a process runs under
the Visual Studio debugger (Start Debugging / F5), the operating system
delivers exceptions to the debugger first, and the top-level
unhandled-exception path Crashpad relies on is bypassed while a debugger
is present (\c IsDebuggerPresent). A genuine crash therefore breaks into
the debugger instead of producing a minidump - the facility still arms
normally, but no dump is written.

To exercise real-crash capture from Visual Studio, launch without the
debugger (Start Without Debugging / Ctrl+F5) or start the application
from \c startMitkWorkbench_release.bat.

Linux and macOS reach the same outcome by a different route. There is no
debugger-detection bypass there; Crashpad's handler is an ordinary signal
handler, and gdb and lldb stop on a fatal signal before the process sees
it. The crash lands in the debugger and the handler never runs. Passing
the signal through (<tt>handle SIGSEGV nostop noprint pass</tt> in gdb)
does let the dump be written, but the simpler way to capture a real crash
is to run the binary directly.

The on-demand and watchdog snapshots use Crashpad's DumpWithoutCrash on
every platform, which goes through neither path, so those are still
written under a debugger; only genuine crashes are intercepted.

\section CrashDumpFacilityPage_Testing Testing

\c MitkCrashHandling ships unit tests for the database logic
(\c mitkCrashDumpDatabaseTest: scan ordering, retention pruning, the
acknowledgment watermark), unit tests for the freeze detector with short
fake timeouts (\c mitkHeartbeatMonitorTest: stall, recovery, and the
per-episode capture bound, no crash handler needed), and integration tests
that spawn a helper executable (\c mitkCrashDumpCaptureTest). The helper
really crashes (modes \c segv / \c abort / \c stackoverflow) to assert the
handler wrote a dump; captures an on-demand snapshot (\c snapshot) to assert
it is written but excluded from the surfacable set, and two in a row
(\c snapshot-twice) to assert a session is not limited to one; and drives the
watchdog
in both directions (\c freeze / \c freeze-recover) to assert a hard-killed
freeze leaves a provisional dump while a recovered one leaves none. The suite
also walks the full facility cycle headlessly after a real crash - arm, list,
acknowledge, delete - i.e. the operations the next-start dialog drives without
any UI. Because these tests only pass when arming succeeds, they also pin the
handler filename: a mismatch between the shipped file and the \c handler_path
the facility sets would make them fail. Further cases cover the settings
(an unarmed facility still lists and deletes, <tt>enabled=false</tt> blocks
arming, retention follows <tt>maxDumpsPerKind</tt>), the dump kinds, the
exclusion of the running session's provisional snapshots, and the run info a
real crash dump and a snapshot carry.

\c MitkAppUtil adds \c mitkCrashDumpSessionOptionsTest for the start-up
precedence and \c QmitkCrashDumpUiTest for the list widget, the manager and
the next-start dialog, including the <i>File report...</i> button with and
without a registered mitk::ICrashReportService.

Those tests spawn a plain helper executable, so what they pin is the runtime
output directory. Each application additionally gets a
<tt>mitkCrashHandlerLayout_&lt;app&gt;</tt> test that runs the handler with
\c --version at the location that application will look for it - the one
requirement that differs per platform, and the only one an application bundle
exercises.

On platforms where the handler cannot arm (e.g. ptrace restrictions in
containers), the tests fail hard unless the environment explicitly allows
skipping by setting \c MITK_CRASHTEST_ALLOW_SKIP, in which case they exit with
the ctest skip code 77. No CI configuration change is needed: the tests are
auto-discovered, and \c MITK_USE_sentry is on by default (only the PythonWheel
and Minimal build configurations disable it).

The in-application paths automation cannot reach - the next-start dialog
after a real crash, the "Capture diagnostics" button on the top-level error
dialog, and the watchdog against a live event loop - are exercised manually
with the \c org.mitk.gui.qt.crashtest plugin. Its Crash Test view offers one
button per failure path: a hard crash (null-pointer write on the UI thread),
an unhandled mitk::Exception (reaches the safe-mode error dialog), and a UI
freeze of configurable duration (run the application with
<tt>--MITK.ui-watchdog 5</tt> to watch the watchdog react in seconds rather
than at a realistic timeout). The view also lists the dumps in the database,
including the snapshot areas the next-start dialog never shows - captures are
thereby observable in-session, before a clean shutdown purges provisional
snapshots or a dialog discard removes crash dumps. The plugin is a QA aid
only: it is off by default
(\c MITK_BUILD_org.mitk.gui.qt.crashtest), no shipping build configuration
includes it, and a deliberate-crash surface must never be part of a released
application.

\section CrashDumpFacilityPage_Symbols Release debug symbols

A stripped Release binary produces undecodable dumps. The option
\c MITK_RELEASE_DEBUG_SYMBOLS (default ON, forwarded from the superbuild)
makes a Release build emit debug symbols for MITK's own code - PDBs on MSVC,
split DWARF on GCC/Clang, dSYM on macOS - so release dumps can be walked back
to function, file, and line. It is deliberately independent of
\c MITK_USE_sentry: the symbols are equally useful for dumps captured by other
means.

There is no execution-speed cost: the Release optimization level and inlining
are preserved. (The MSVC binary is marginally larger - with \c /DEBUG present
the linker's \c /OPT:ICF folds slightly fewer identical functions, to keep
them individually debuggable - but this is a code-size, not a runtime-speed,
difference.)

- MSVC compiles with \c /Z7 (CodeView embedded per object, codegen-identical
  to no debug info and parallel-build safe, unlike \c /Zi) while keeping
  \c /O2 \c /Ob2 (full inlining, unlike RelWithDebInfo's \c /Ob1). The link
  step uses \c /DEBUG:FULL (a self-contained, shippable PDB) and restores
  \c /OPT:REF \c /OPT:ICF, which \c /DEBUG would otherwise disable.
- GCC/Clang use \c -gsplit-dwarf, which is codegen-neutral and needs no
  link-side flag; macOS uses \c -g plus \c dsymutil at archive time.

The flags ride the \c MitkCompilerFlags interface target, which only code
compiled by MITK's own module/plugin/application machinery links. Prebuilt
third-party dependencies (ITK, VTK, Boost, Qt, ...) are built separately under
the superbuild \c ep prefix and never see these flags, so they stay
symbol-free. Bundled sources under \c Utilities/ that are compiled through the
MITK machinery do receive symbols (they are MITK-built and linked into MITK
libraries); this is harmless and still cost-free. The practical consequence:
a crash inside a prebuilt third-party library resolves to
function-name-plus-offset only, while MITK frames resolve to file and line.

\subsection CrashDumpFacilityPage_Symbols_Archive Archiving symbols

The installers do not ship the symbols. On Windows the debug info lives in
separate PDBs that are never installed; on Linux and macOS the binaries are
not stripped (see \ref PackagingPage), so they keep their symbol tables while
split DWARF holds the bulk of the debug info outside them. Either way the full
symbols must be archived separately, by the \c package-symbols target:

\code
cmake --build . --config Release --target package-symbols
\endcode

This writes <tt>&lt;CPACK_PACKAGE_FILE_NAME&gt;-symbols.zip</tt> (for example
<tt>MITK-2025.12.99-windows-x86_64-symbols.zip</tt>). Keep the archive that
matches a given release; a dump is only decodable against the symbols built
from the exact same sources. Producing and archiving this file for every
released build is a release-pipeline responsibility - see \ref PackagingPage.

\subsection CrashDumpFacilityPage_Symbols_HowTo Symbolicating a dump

Walking a dump back to function, file, and line is a per-OS operation and needs
the symbols archive that matches the exact build. The step-by-step workflow for
Windows, Linux, and macOS - including how to read the result and how to
reproduce a dump for practice - is a separate developer how-to:
\subpage AnalyzingCrashDumpsHowTo.

\section CrashDumpFacilityPage_MixedConfigs Debug and Release in one superbuild tree

The static sentry/crashpad libraries of both configurations coexist in the
superbuild's \c ep prefix (Debug carries the usual \c d postfix). The
\c crashpad_handler executable has no such postfix: whichever configuration
installed last provides <tt>ep/bin/crashpad_handler(.exe)</tt>. For running
and testing this is harmless - the handler is a standalone tool and writes
identical dumps - but before packaging a release, make sure the sentry
external project was (re)installed in Release, or the package ships a Debug
handler that depends on debug CRT libraries.
*/
